  shared module has to pick one, and every form plus every test will encode against that choice.

  - [1mES modules[0m — [38;5;153mvalidation.js[39m exports functions; [38;5;153mindex.html[39m switches to [38;5;153m<script type="module" src="app.js">[39m.
    Browser-native, no bundler, and the same file imports cleanly into a Node test runner. Unifies the split going
    forward. Costs you one real thing: [38;5;153mfile://[39m can't load ES modules (CORS), so opening [38;5;153mindex.html[39m by double-clicking
    stops working — you'd need [38;5;153mnpx serve[39m or any static server. If you currently develop by double-clicking the file,
    that's a daily papercut, not a theoretical one.
  - [1mBrowser global[0m — [38;5;153mvalidation.js[39m sets [38;5;153mwindow.Validation[39m, loaded via a second [38;5;153m<script>[39m before [38;5;153mapp.js[39m. Zero friction,
    [38;5;153mfile://[39m keeps working, matches [38;5;153mapp.js[39m exactly as written. Costs you: global namespace collisions, load-order
    dependence between script tags, and it's awkward to unit-test in Node without a shim — which matters because there's
    no test setup here at all yet.
  - [1mCommonJS[0m — matches [38;5;153msrc/[39m, tests trivially in Node, but browsers can't load it without a bundler, so this one drags a
    build step into a repo that currently has zero dependencies.

  Cheap to change later: adding files in whichever format you pick. Expensive: switching format after several forms
  import the module, since every consumer and every test changes with it. CommonJS is the one that changes your
  dependency story permanently.

  [1mMy recommendation: ES modules.[0m It's the only option that's both browser-loadable with no build and Node-testable with
  no shim, and given there's no test infrastructure yet, I don't want to pick a format that makes the first test harder
  to write. The [38;5;153mfile://[39m loss is the honest cost.
